  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nCurrently-Friday... KMLB radar imagery shows scattered to \nnumerous east moving showers and lightning storms at about 20mph\nwith most of the showers and lightning storms south of the \nOrlando Metro; Much of the activity has pushed offshore of the \nBrevard and Indian River county coasts. GOES-16 satellite imagery \nand local observations indicate cloudy skies with stratocumulus \nand cirrus clouds across east central Florida. Analysis charts \nshow a stationary boundary over the deep south with a shortwave \nover northern Florida, in addition to, northern east central \nFlorida. The Atlantic ridge axis is well to the east-southeast of \neast central Florida over the Atlantic waters. Temperatures are \nare rain cooled across east central Florida and are currently in \nthe mid 70s to mid 80s. Dew points are in the low to upper 70s. \nGradient winds are generally from the WSW at 10-15mph with gusts \nto 20-25mph. \n\nRain chances are expected to diminish after around 4PM with mostly\ndry conditions into the evening hours. Model guidance has varied\nquite a bit once again for rain chances and the timing of showers\nand lightning storms into tomorrow. However, I've adjusted the\nforecast towards the rain chances and timing that is closer to \nwhat we had today since the stationary boundary is expected to \nremain over the southern US, as well as the shortwave over \nnorthern Florida and the adjacent Atlantic waters. Mostly dry \nweather is forecast overnight, with rain chances increasing into \nthe morning and afternoon hours on Friday. With that said, \nscattered to numerous (60-70% chance) east- southeast moving \nshowers and lightning storms are forecast to develop from \nnorthwest to southeast across east central Florida into the late \nmorning and afternoon hours. Rain chances lower into the evening \nhours and overnight. MLCAPE up to 1,000-3,600 J/kg, 0-6km shear up\nto 20-40kts, and PVA traversing east central Florida will support\nthe potential for isolated to scattered strong storms capable of \nproducing wind gusts up to 50mph, frequent to excessive lightning,\nand heavy rainfall (1-3\" with a 5% chance of totals up to 4-5\"). \nA Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall Exists for much of east \ncentral Florida (5% chance). Near to below normal highs in the \nupper 80s to low 90s are forecast with heat index values up to \n100F-105F degrees, as well as lows in the low to mid 70s are \nforecast. He\n\nSaturday-Sunday... The active weather pattern is expected to \ncontinue through the weekend with a shortwave over the deep south\nand eastern Gulf. Rain chances (70-80%) are expected to remain \nhigh and above normal through the weekend with PWATS in excess of \n2.0\" each day. Flow is expected to veer southerly and gradually \nweaken at around 10mph with gusts to 15mph which will allow a weak\neast coast sea breeze to develop mainly south of Cape Canaveral \non Sunday. Scattered to widespread showers and lightning storms \nare forecast into each afternoon and evening with the highest rain\nchances north of the Treasure Coast on Saturday and from Cape \nCanaveral southwards on Sunday. Afternoon highs in the upper 80s\nto low 90s with lows in the low to mid 70s are forecast. \n\nMonday-Wednesday... Near normal rain chances (generally 40-70% \nchance) are expected into next week with the Atlantic ridge axis \nforecast to build over southern Florida. Winds are forecast to\nback SSW on Monday before backing easterly on Wednesday. The east\ncoast sea breeze is forecast strengthen through midweek and back\nmore easterly through Wednesday. Scattered showers and lightning\nstorms are forecast to develop each afternoon which will be\ncapable of frequent lightning, gusty winds, and heavy rainfall. \nTemperatures are expected to warm slightly with highs in the low \n90s and lows in the mid to upper 70s. A Moderate HeatRisk is \nforecast.",
